new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a2cc012
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/SWITCH.md
@@ -0,0 +1,281 @@
+# Nintendo Switch 1 cycle runner (experimental)
+
+An opt-in libnx/SDL2 host for one compiled cartridge per `.nro`. It reuses the
+cycle CPU/PPU/APU/mappers, scheduler, ROM checks, session lifecycle, state
+serialization and battery saves. Desktop source selection remains unchanged.
+This is an experimental prototype. The first physical-console test confirmed
+boot, demo playback, controls and music, but measured about 28 FPS and a save
+failure at the 30-second automatic checkpoint. The second user test measured
+46.36 FPS overall over 5324 frames / 114.838 seconds, with zero saves, zero
+dropped audio chunks and normal exit. Full-speed operation remains unfinished.
+
+In 0.2, `NESRECOMP_SWITCH_SAVES` defaults OFF. The Mario recipe selects HLE
+PPU, while framework/desktop defaults remain LLE. Switch Release targets use
+`-O3` and link-time optimization; the host requests VSync off and logs per-stage
+frame timings every five seconds. No clocks or gameplay timing are changed.
+
+## Scope
+
+`NESRECOMP_SWITCH_VERSION` supplies both the NACP display version and the
+runtime startup banner through `CYC_SWITCH_VERSION`. Callers should pass their
+release version explicitly; changing it regenerates metadata and recompiles
+the banner. The frontend does not maintain a version counter. A calling
+workspace may own per-game versions and deployment policy separately.
+
+The frontend remains independent of game recipes. Optional shared
+[hardware profiles and static modules](CYCLE_PROFILES.md) live in the cycle
+build API, while ROM revision, seeds, renderer selection and title-specific
+tests belong in the calling game project. These static extensions do not
+enable Switch `HOST_EXTRAS` or runtime mods, and the initial checked NROM
+profile introduces no new execution shortcuts.
+
+The first target is vanilla Super Mario Bros., mapper 0. Use the standalone
+`runner/cyc/project`, not the enhanced Mario CMake target. The Switch build
+rejects `HEADLESS`, `MODS`, `HD_PACKS`, `HOST_EXTRAS` and explicit `BIOS` inputs.
+It disables recomp-ui and diagnostic replay recording and does not build the
+desktop TCP/chat/settings frontend. Generic dependency-free mod registries
+remain linked as part of the shared core, with no mod catalog or gameplay mods.
+FDS, light guns, multiple players and enhanced game hosts are outside this
+initial frontend's supported scope.
+
+Recompilation coverage is not a performance guarantee. Uncovered instructions
+can use the existing interpreter fallback. Measure native/interpreted cycles,
+frame times and audio behavior on hardware at stock clocks before claiming
+full-speed play. The generic default PPU remains LLE; the Mario performance
+recipe explicitly selects HLE. No timing enhancement is enabled.
+
+## Verified tools
+
+- Host: Apple Silicon macOS 26.5.1, Apple Clang 17.0.0, CMake 4.5.0,
+  Ninja 1.13.2, Python 3.14.3 for the recorded test commands.
+- Host SDL: Homebrew sdl2-compat 2.32.74 (SDL2 CMake API).
+- Cross SDK: official ARM64 Linux devkitPro Docker image,
+  `devkitpro/devkita64@sha256:1fc388c3a0d34bd2045a6dadcb1020e069d5f876a187fd705de14b4440c00282`.
+- Cross packages: devkitA64 r29.2-1 (GCC 15.2.0), libnx 4.12.0-1,
+  switch-tools 1.13.1-1, switch-cmake 1.5.2-1, switch-sdl2 2.28.5-4.
+
+These are tested versions, not established minimum versions. Native macOS
+devkitPro installation was unavailable during this validation. Its package
+manager requires administrator installation; follow the official
+[devkitPro pacman instructions](https://github.com/devkitPro/pacman/releases).
+Install `switch-dev` and `switch-sdl2`. Check the installed compiler with `file`
+and `--version` rather than assuming Rosetta is necessary.
+
+## Native Mac generation and cross-compilation
+
+Run from the NESRecomp root. Keep all private ROMs/generated code in ignored
+locations. The example expects an existing Mario checkout in `$SMB_REPO` and
+your matching original ROM at the absolute `$NESRECOMP_SMB_ROM` path.
+
+```sh
+cmake -S recompiler -B build/recompiler -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
+cmake --build build/recompiler --parallel 4
+build/recompiler/NESRecomp --help
+python3 "$SMB_REPO/tools/verify_usa_rom.py" --rom "$NESRECOMP_SMB_ROM" \
+  --crc D445F698 --mapper 0 --title 'Super Mario Bros.'
+mkdir -p build/smb1
+cat > build/smb1/vanilla.toml <<'EOF'
+[game]
+output_prefix = "smb1-vanilla"
+cycle_accurate = true
+EOF
+cmake -S runner/cyc/project -B build/smb1/host -G Ninja \
+  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DNESRECOMP_HEADLESS=ON -DNESRECOMP_RECOMP_UI=OFF \
+  -DNESRECOMP_ROM="$NESRECOMP_SMB_ROM" \
+  -DNESRECOMP_GAME_CONFIG="$PWD/build/smb1/vanilla.toml" \
+  -DNESRECOMP_CYCLE_SEEDS="$SMB_REPO/cyc_seeds.txt" \
+  -DNESRECOMP_HOST_COMPILER="$PWD/build/recompiler/NESRecomp"
+cmake --build build/smb1/host --parallel 4
+build/smb1/host/nes_game "$NESRECOMP_SMB_ROM" --frames 600 \
+  --screenshot "$PWD/build/smb1/boot.png"
+```
+
+With a native macOS SDK installed:
+
+```sh
+export DEVKITPRO=/opt/devkitpro
+export DEVKITA64="$DEVKITPRO/devkitA64"
+export PATH="$DEVKITA64/bin:$DEVKITPRO/tools/bin:$DEVKITPRO/portlibs/switch/bin:$PATH"
+cmake -S runner/cyc/project -B build/smb1/switch -G Ninja \
+  -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE="$DEVKITPRO/cmake/Switch.cmake" \
+  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DNESRECOMP_RECOMP_UI=OFF \
+  -DNESRECOMP_PPU_IMPL=HLE -DNESRECOMP_SWITCH_SAVES=OFF \
+  -DNESRECOMP_SWITCH_LTO=ON -DNESRECOMP_SWITCH_VERSION=0.2.0 \
+  -DNESRECOMP_HOST_COMPILER="$PWD/build/recompiler/NESRecomp" \
+  -DNESRECOMP_ROM="$NESRECOMP_SMB_ROM" \
+  -DNESRECOMP_GAME_CONFIG="$PWD/build/smb1/vanilla.toml" \
+  -DNESRECOMP_CYCLE_SEEDS="$SMB_REPO/cyc_seeds.txt" \
+  -DNESRECOMP_SWITCH_GAME_DIR=sdmc:/switch/nesrecomp/smb1 \
+  '-DNESRECOMP_SWITCH_TITLE=Super Mario Bros. Recomp'
+cmake --build build/smb1/switch --parallel 4
+# build/smb1/switch/nes_game.nro and nes_game.nacp
+```
+
+The user-supplied Mario ROM (audited CRC D445F698, mapper 0) now passes native
+generation, a 600-frame title boot and a separate 1800-frame input route into
+World 1-1. Native/interpreter per-frame hashes match; reloading a state saved
+after zero-based frame 600 reproduces all remaining 1199 frame hashes and the
+final screenshot. A Mario NRO was produced with the Docker SDK; its NRO/NACP
+headers were checked. All nine generated C units match between Mac and Linux
+host compilers. No mods, HD packs, recomp-ui or netplay were enabled.
+The code generator executable must be runnable by the build host. A Linux
+container cannot execute the Mac's Mach-O compiler; use the prepared fixture
+below, or compile a separate Linux host compiler inside that container.
+
+The SDK's real `nx_generate_nacp` and `nx_create_nro` helpers package metadata
+and the NRO. `find_package(SDL2 CONFIG REQUIRED)` prevents headless fallback.
+Runtime game-directory and NACP title/author/version are CMake inputs, so each
+game can have separate paths and metadata without platform-source edits.
+
+## ROM-free verification and Docker cross-build (executed)
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/cyc/test_switch_host.py --out build/switch-host-contract \
+  --recompiler build/recompiler/NESRecomp
+docker run --rm -v "$PWD:$PWD" -w "$PWD" \
+  devkitpro/devkita64@sha256:1fc388c3a0d34bd2045a6dadcb1020e069d5f876a187fd705de14b4440c00282 \
+  bash -c 'cmake -S build/switch-host-contract/project -B build/switch-cross-fixture -G Ninja \
+    -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/opt/devkitpro/cmake/Switch.cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && \
+    cmake --build build/switch-cross-fixture --parallel 4'
+```
+
+The test generates its own small NROM program and compiles it on the Mac. It
+executes the Switch SDL host with dummy video/audio drivers, verifies 100%
+native ROM coverage, and tests autosave/resume, missing/wrong ROM rejection,
+corrupt-state preservation, save-write errors and an invalid save directory.
+It also emits a cross-build harness containing the same Mac-generated C,
+shared core source list and Switch host. The Docker mount preserves absolute
+generated paths. Output: `build/switch-cross-fixture/switch_fixture.nro`.
+
+The normal `runner/cyc/project` cross-build was independently exercised using
+a Linux host compiler in the same image. Mac/Linux generated fixture sources
+matched byte for byte. NRO0 header, asset section, NACP size and title were
+checked on the resulting NRO. No Nintendo game data is used by these tests.
+
+Desktop regressions: five CTests passed (`cyc_helper_test`, `cyc_mapper_test`,
+`cyc_frame_pacing_test`, `cyc_input_test`, `cyc_window_run`); the existing
+`test_cyc_project.py` passed generation/rebuild/identity checks. Set
+`SDL2_DIR=/opt/homebrew/opt/sdl2-compat/lib/cmake/SDL2` explicitly for the
+standalone desktop test build: its top-level SDL search can otherwise select
+the bundled Windows DLL on this Mac. This existing test-build issue was not
+changed as part of the Switch patch.
+
+## SD layout and controls
+
+The default 0.2 build starts fresh and does not read or write progress. ZL/ZR
+do nothing, and L + R + Plus exits without saving. Existing save files remain
+untouched. The save controls/layout below describe the optional
+`NESRECOMP_SWITCH_SAVES=ON` implementation retained for debugging; it failed
+on the first physical SD test and is not enabled by the Mario build recipe.
+
+Logging remains active. Every five seconds `runtime.log` reports FPS and
+average emulation, audio, presentation and wait milliseconds. Startup names
+the renderer, PPU and save setting for the next physical-console measurement.
+The `audio_supply` report adds generated PCM duration relative to wall time,
+queued output duration and software queue observations. Sustained supply below
+one means the producer cannot feed real-time playback. Empty queue observations
+are not exact hardware underrun counts; the existing dropped-chunk counter
+tracks excess output discarded at the upper limit, not starvation.
+
+For an explicit development launch, `--nxlink-log` connects stdout to the
+nxlink host while keeping stderr's SD log. Launch it using nxlink's `-s` server
+and `--args '--nxlink-log --exit-after 1800'` (the frame limit is optional).
+Host timing/audio reports are mirrored to both destinations. A failed or
+unavailable connection falls back to the SD log. Ordinary launches do not
+initialize sockets. Network diagnostics also report applet/operation/performance
+mode and attempt a read-only CPU clock query; clock-service failures are logged
+and no clocks are changed. Docker users must publish console TCP port 28771,
+in addition to reaching the console's NetLoader TCP port 28280 for upload.
+The native SDL contract covers metrics and non-Switch network fallback;
+physical network/service behavior needs console qualification.
+
+For the Mario recipe, place:
+
+```text
+sdmc:/switch/nesrecomp/smb1/
+  SuperMarioBrosRecomp.nro     # renamed nes_game.nro
+  rom.nes                     # your matching original ROM
+  runtime.log                 # created each launch
+  saves/
+    auto.cycstate             # automatic resume
+    slot.cycstate             # explicit quick save
+    rom.sav                   # battery RAM, only if the cartridge has it
+```
+
+The build-machine ROM location is never used to locate the runtime ROM. Core
+cartridge/PRG identity validation remains in the original host. The ROM is
+read-only. Directories are created recursively; invalid directories and save
+errors fail visibly rather than silently continuing. There is no persistent
+frontend config in this first host; controls and display policy are fixed.
+
+- D-pad or left stick: move; physical Nintendo A/B: NES A/B.
+- Plus/Minus: Start/Select.
+- ZL: quick save; ZR: quick load. A missing/invalid quick-load slot reports an
+  error and returns to homebrew without replacing that slot.
+- L + R + Plus: save and quit to the homebrew launcher.
+- Hold physical Nintendo X at launch: intentionally start fresh rather than
+  loading the automatic state. Exiting then replaces the automatic state.
+
+Normally the automatic state loads at launch. Checkpoints are written every
+30 seconds, on SDL background/focus-loss events, on libnx exit/focus hooks,
+and at normal exit. libnx hooks run between emulated frames. A corrupt or
+incompatible automatic state is preserved and startup stops. Back up saves
+before updating the executable; the core does not promise state portability
+between builds or platforms. Checkpoint writes cannot guarantee the last
+moment survives a power cut or forced process termination.
+
+Video uses a 256x240 texture, nearest filtering, a 4:3 displayed image and
+letterboxing. Audio is generated at 48 kHz mono and converted with an SDL
+audio stream to the obtained device format/rate/channels. The device queue
+is bounded to about 100 ms; dropped audio chunks and coverage counters are
+recorded in `runtime.log`. Guest deadlines use the core's frame duration,
+independent of display VSync.
+
+## Hardware validation still required
+
+Boot and play Mario's first level; inspect missing-ROM/save-error screens;
+verify physical buttons, controller reconnection, handheld/docked rendering,
+long-session audio and frame times, HOME/sleep/close and subsequent resume.
+These were unverified during initial implementation. The later user test
+confirmed boot/demo, Plus to start, controls and music, but also slow execution
+and the SD save failure. The 0.2 user log shows sustained 45–49 FPS after
+startup, about 20.07 ms emulation, 1.40 ms presentation and 0.025 ms audio per
+frame. Emulation needs about 24% less CPU time to fit the 60.10 FPS budget.
+The 115-second run exited normally without saving or dropping audio chunks;
+long-session stability and suspend/resume still need hardware testing.
+SDL dummy tests establish host logic, not working Switch GPU/audio devices.
+The actual Mario Switch host also ran with dummy video/audio on the Mac:
+600-frame boot, automatic resume and another 600 frames passed. A paced run
+completed 600 frames in about 9.98 seconds but dropped 14 audio chunks under
+the dummy backend; physical-device audio stability is not established.
+
+A TriCNES comparison matched CPU/bus traces, registers, cycle counts, memory
+and raw 9-bit pixel hashes on all 1800 scripted frames. Internal hardware
+hashes and the RGB palette conversion differ, so this is not full oracle
+parity. The oracle needed `-Wno-c++11-narrowing` with Apple Clang to build;
+no oracle source was changed.
+
+The 0.2 HLE + LTO candidate matches the LLE baseline over a separate 1800-frame
+Mario route: traced hashes, untraced memory/raw-pixel captures, PCM and final
+screenshots agree. HLE kernel/native-interval/raster CTests pass, including
+4096 varied raster cases. No-save frontend tests run 1900 paced frames with an
+invalid save directory and old state, with no progress writes or save errors.
+The same no-save Mario frontend runs on Mac dummy drivers and preserves its
+old state file. Order-balanced Mac benchmarks measured about 25% less process
+CPU time with HLE + Clang LTO; this is not a measured Switch speedup.
+
+An NRO appears in the homebrew launcher. Individual Switch HOME-menu icons
+require a separately configured forwarder; this patch does not create one.
+Mapper support alone does not establish a game's full playability.
+
+A calling workspace now has a European Adventure Island Classic module using
+explicit PAL timing and the generic CNROM core. Its desktop LLE/HLE first-level
+routes match in native/interpreter execution, state continuation, raw frames
+and PCM; physical-console qualification is pending. No PAL/CNROM native
+interval shortcuts were enabled. The frontend logs effective region and target
+FPS so a PAL title's approximately 50 FPS target is visible.
+
+Optional [core-clock sampling](CORE_PROFILER.md) is enabled with
+`NESRECOMP_CORE_PROFILER=ON`, default OFF. It reports estimated device-clock
+costs alongside ordinary frame timings; diagnostic overhead means those FPS
+figures are not production benchmarks. It has no game-specific branches.
